  "resultText": "A clinic cancellation with `refund: true` now refunds the no-show invoice, and `npm test` passes (36 unit tests and 7 e2e tests). Typecheck and lint are clean too.\n\n- **Cancelling after a no-show:** the domain previously allowed cancelling only `SCHEDULED` appointments, so a no-show appointment couldn't be cancelled at all. I made `Appointment.cancel` also accept `NO_SHOW`, but only when the reason is `clinic`. Any other reason on a no-show still throws.\n- **Request and event:** `POST /scheduling/appointments/:id/cancel` now reads `refund` from the body, defaulting to `false`. `AppointmentCancelled` carries a new `refund` field.\n- **Refund in billing:** billing's scheduling listener now handles `AppointmentCancelled`. When the reason is `clinic` and `refund` is true, it marks that appointment's no-show invoice as `REFUNDED` and records `refundedAt`.\n- **No-op cases:** nothing happens if there is no no-show invoice, for example a clinic cancellation of a still-scheduled appointment. Nothing happens either if the invoice is already refunded.\n- **Schema and persistence:** I added a nullable `refundedAt` column to `BillingInvoice` in `prisma/schema.prisma`. The invoice repository's `save` now updates an existing invoice instead of always creating one. The invoices API response includes `refundedAt`. The e2e setup runs `prisma db push`, so the new column is applied there. Any other database needs the same push.\n- **Tests:** I added unit tests for the domain rules, the invoicing service and the appointments service. Three e2e cases cover the three paths:\n  - a no-show followed by a clinic cancel with refund leaves the invoice `REFUNDED`;\n  - the same cancel without `refund` leaves it `OPEN`;\n  - a clinic cancel with refund on a still-scheduled appointment succeeds with no invoice.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
